Falsifies `kernel::arch::x86_64::amd_vi::tests::two_devices_behind_one_alias_never_share_a_domain`.

Architecture: x86_64
Environment: NIFE_IOMMU=amd

The defect: `attach` ignores a conflict and fails closed only when it has no slot to record an
alias in. A second device behind the same bridge then writes its domain into the alias entry the
first device's DMA arrives under, which is what the driver did before provisional milestone 767
(AMD-Vi hardening before the first AMD boot): the first device's DMA moves into the second's
domain with nothing printed.

Expected to fail: `assert_ne!(held[0] & DTE_ROOT_MASK, b, "the second device took the first's
alias")`. Measured on patagonia 2026-10-05 (UTC), QEMU 11.1.1, on the runner's `pci-bridge` alias
range (bus 1 aliased to 0x0018).

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/amd_vi.rs b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/amd_vi.rs
index 40fd2ee15..11507adbc 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/amd_vi.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/amd_vi.rs
@@ -781,7 +781,7 @@ pub fn attach(rid: u32, root: u64) {
         .find_map(|x| u.holder(x).filter(|&h| h != rid).map(|h| (x, h)));
     // A new alias needs a slot to record its holder in; without one, fail closed as for a conflict.
     let out_of_slots = alias.is_some_and(|a| u.holder(a) != Some(rid) && !u.alias_slot_free());
-    if conflict.is_some() || out_of_slots {
+    if out_of_slots {
         for x in ids.into_iter().flatten() {
             u.block(x);
         }
